# Design: Resume attempts do not hold exclusive scope

## Problem
After a controller restart, initializeState retains mid-run
attempts as phase "resume". compatible() skipped only done,
blocked, and abandoned attempts, so a retained attempt vetoed
every task with an overlapping plan scope, including the task
of the other retained attempt. Two overlapping retained
attempts deadlocked dispatch: the run loop spun at zero
workers with no error and no event.

## Approach
Skip "resume" attempts in compatible(). Exclusivity is not
lost: start() reuses the retained attempt and sets its phase
to "running" synchronously, before the dispatch loop evaluates
the next candidate, so later overlapping candidates are vetoed
from that moment. Ready() already sorts "doing" tasks ahead of
"todo", so retained work keeps dispatch priority and fresh
tasks cannot jump ahead of it.

## Key Trade-offs
- Overlapping retained attempts now resume serially instead of
  never; the second waits for the first to integrate.
- A retained attempt whose task is not ready (blocked
  dependencies) no longer vetoes overlapping fresh work. That
  work can proceed; prepare() later merges the retained result
  onto the moved tip.

## Alternatives Considered
- Reorder the dispatch loop to offer retained tasks first —
  rejected: Ready() already orders "doing" first.
- Rebase retained worktrees to the current tip during resume —
  rejected: prepare() already merges a stale-base result commit
  onto the check base.

